"audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob08aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "case-sensitive"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "with the capacity of detecting capital letters and lowercase"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You just need to construe few parameters such as the name, the value and if it is case-sensitive or case-insensitive."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob08aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "purely"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "basically, entirely"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: A constant is global purely because it can be used all across the script."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob08aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "memorize "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to remember perfectly"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Strings and functions\u2026 I'm supposed to memorize every single one."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob08aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "harsh "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "referring to severe or hard to deal with"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For what you're telling me, he seems to be pretty harsh"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob08aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Search for information about constants in PHP. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a list of the most common constants and their functions. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Prepare a presentation where you explain the most important constants in PHP. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your presentation with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I understand what a constant in PHP is.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I know how constants are created. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I express why constants are considered global.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my work with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Responses With So"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When we want to agree with a person or to say that we do or like the same thing, we use responses with <strong>so.</strong>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Depending on the tense you use, your answers will have the main auxiliary of that tense. </br></br>  Examples: </br></br>  <strong>Present:</strong> </br><i> A: I <strong>have</strong> to study constants in PHP. </br> B: So <strong>do</strong> I. (I also have to study.)</br></br>  A: I'<strong>m</strong> certain that constants are easier than strings.  </br> B: So <strong>am</strong> I. (I am also certain that constants are easier than strings.) </i> </br></br>   <strong>Past Simple:</strong></br><i> A: I <strong>used</strong> the define() function to create a constant. </br> B: So d<strong>id</strong> I. ( I also used the define() function to create a constant) </br></br>  A: I <strong>was</strong> sure that constants were global. </br> B: So <strong>was</strong> I. (I was also sure that constants were global.)</i></br></br>  <strong>Future</strong>:</br><i> A: Next week, I <strong>will go</strong> to a conference about constants in PHP. </br> B: Really? So <strong>will</strong> I. ( I also will go to a conference about constants in PHP)  </i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "<strong>Nataly:</strong> For me, constants are the easiest identifiers in PHP. I love working with them. </br> <strong>Mark:</strong> So do I. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "<strong>Nataly:</strong> At the beginning, I had some problems understanding why constant were global. </br> <strong>Mark:</strong> Really? So did I."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Be Supposed To"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use the expression <strong>be supposed to</strong> when you want to talk about actions or events that you or someone expect to happen."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Use this expression to talk about actions that people are expecting from you, or that are always true.</br>  You can follow this structure to use be supposed to:</br></br>  Subject + <strong>am/is/are</strong> + <strong>supposed to</strong> + verb + complement.</br></br>  Example:</br><i> Students are supposed to come to class until November 17th</br> Employees are not supposed to be late to any meeting.</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, I'm supposed to memorize every single detail. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Constants are supposed to be like variables, but these are identifiers created for simple values."}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob08re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Nataly:</strong> What are you doing?</br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> I'm creating some constants to finish today's assignment.</br>\n<strong>Nataly:</strong> For me, constants are the easiest identifiers in PHP. I love working with them. </br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> So do I. After studying strings and their functions, I absolutely prefer constants.</br>\n<strong>Nataly:</strong> Yeah! Constants are supposed to be like variables, but these are identifiers created for simple values. Plus, when constants are defined, you cannot change them later in the script. </br>"}, {"time": "34000", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob08re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Mark:</strong> I totally agree with you. To create a constant, you just need to construe of few parameters such as the name, the value and if it is case-sensitive or case-insensitive; then, you are done.</br>\n<strong>Nataly:</strong> What are the function of those parameters? </br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> Well, the name and the value permit to specify and determine the correct name of the constant and its value. </br>\n<strong>Nataly:</strong> And, what about the function of constant? </br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> You use define() to create it. This function permits to store data, while the script is being executed. </br>\n<strong>Nataly:</strong> You really know about it! At the beginning, I had some problems understanding why constant were global. </br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> Really? So did I. Indeed, I had a teacher who explained to me that a constant is global purely because it can be used all across the script."}, {"time": "90000", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob08re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Nataly:</strong> I hadn't thought about that connection before, but it makes a lot of sense.</br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> Yes! </br>\n<strong>Nataly:</strong> Well, I'm supposed to memorize every single detail.</br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> Really? That's almost impossible!</br>\n<strong>Nataly:</strong> Try telling that to my teacher.</br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> For what you're telling me, he seems to be pretty harsh. </br>\n<strong>Nataly:</strong> He is supposed to be the best at college, but I prefer inclusive teachers.</br>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> Well, so do I."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n6le02ob08re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le02ob08re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 2, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can say what a constant in PHP is.   "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can describe how to create a constant.   "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I 